speechtext:
For a return showing:-
1.	The total number of permanent employees of the Department of the Interior who were compulsorily retired between the 1st of January, 1931. to date;
2.	The total number of such employees who receive or will receive superannuation allowances;
3.	The total number of such employees who were transferred to other permanent positions in the federal government service at salaries equal to the salaries they were receiving on retirement;
4.	The total number of such employees who were transferred to other permanent positions in the federal government service at salaries less than the salaries they were receiving on retirement;
5.	The total number of such employees who were transferred to temporary positions in the federal government service, and who are still employed temporarily;
6.	The total number of such employees who received gratuities on retirement under the provisions of the Superannuation Act of 1924;
7.	The total number of such employees who received returns of contributions from the retirement fund;
8.	The average amount of money which will have to be paid annually to each of the employees superannuated on compulsory retirement from the Department of the Interior;
9.	The total sum of money paid to such employees from the retirement fund;
10.	The total sum of money paid to such retired employees as gratuities under the Superannuation Act of 1924.

I am wondering if any action will be taken. I think the remarks of the hon. members preceding me have been both moderate and fair. We know about the practice of railways which in the past have constructed lines to certain points, and then left them for long periods of time. That condition requires no rehearsing. In the past it has been a serious scandal in such districts as the Lethbridge district, and others throughout western Canada. In the present instance we have a very striking illustration of the attitude of the railways towards requirements of districts. When the hon. member for Kindersley said that a back haul of about 160 miles was necessary he was understanding the facts-but perhaps wisely so. I find that at the present time to supply Little Gem, Naco, and points east of there, coal is run from Drumheller to Saskatoon, from Saskatoon west to Biggar, and from Biggar west to points of destination, a distance of 370 miles at a charge to the purchaser of about $3.80 a ton. That coal could be laid in there at about $2 a ton if the suggestion of the hon. member for Acadia (Mr. Gardiner) were adopted, and if it were drawn over the now almost completely constructed line, lacking only ballast. From my experience in the western provinces and from my knowledge of the territory, I am satisfied the base upon which the ties are laid is for the most part sufficiently level to enable the hauling of fairly substantial loads without great risk to the lines.
I support the contentions of those hon. members who have preceded me, and in that connection merely wish to say that I have been in communication with the Western Canada Fuel Association, the Drumheller coal operators, the board of trade of Drumheller, the Board of Railway Commisisoners, and the railways. To date I have not been able to get any satisfaction from the railways. The matter however, is still under consideration, and if the minister will add his voice to ours I am satisfied that collectively we may be able to secure some justifiable and satisfactory solution. The conditions at present are grossly unfair. Canada has paid for the line;

the line is ready for use; the people need the service, but through lack of ballast they are being deliberately held up. I hope the minister will join with us in protesting to the railways.
